WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:16.200
Illness is never fun for any of us, but for people living with

2
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.420
diabetes, it can actually become quite dangerous really quickly.

3
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:23.860
So, even something like a mild viral illness can trigger high blood

4
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.770
glucose or hyperglycemia. And when we've got high levels of

5
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.480
glucose in our blood, it can actually drive water and electrolyte loss quite

6
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.290
quickly. This dehydration can make glucose really

7
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.940
hard to control, so regardless of your diabetes

8
00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:42.420
control normally, and it can also alter things like drug

9
00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.449
pharmacokinetics, and can put a real strain on the kidneys,

10
00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.880
even for people without chronic kidney disease.

11
00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.660
Without correction, this can actually escalate really quickly into certain

12
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.100
conditions like DKA or HHS, which stands for diabetic

13
00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:00.860
ketoacidosis or hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state.

14
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.371
And unfortunately, this can actually happen quite quickly in some individuals, and

15
00:01:05.401 --> 00:01:07.220
we don't know when it's going to happen.

16
00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:18.840
A sick day action plan or a sick day management plan is important for every

17
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.820
person living with diabetes. Now, what this plan does is it gives

18
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.560
the person clear written instructions on what to do

19
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.340
in the event of becoming unwell.

20
00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:33.100
It should be personalized to the person, including their diabetes

21
00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:37.100
type, what medications they're on, any sort of comorbidities that they

22
00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.120
might have which affects the sick day plan, as well as their confidence level

23
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.120
or health literacy levels. But it doesn't need to be complicated, and

24
00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.500
there's lots of versions out there.

25
00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.720
Now, a good plan generally covers a few key things, and that

26
00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.720
includes how often and when to check things like glucose or

27
00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:58.480
ketone levels, which medications that an individual should

28
00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:02.480
pause or adjust, what to drink and eat and

29
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:02.760
when,

30
00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.560
and when to seek urgent medical care, so when to escalate into calling

31
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.560
the doctor or heading off to the emergency department or urgent care center.

32
00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.500
Having a sick day plan can actually help prevent hospitalizations

33
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.680
and ensure people stay where they want to stay when they're ill, and that's in the

34
00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:21.800
comfort of their own home.

35
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.440
In my experience working as both a diabetes educator and a pharmacist

36
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:36.400
across tertiary and primary care settings, I've come across very

37
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:40.160
few people that have either had a sick day

38
00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.520
plan or had an up-to-date sick day plan.

39
00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:47.240
How do we realistically actually get patients to have a

40
00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.220
plan and to have a plan that's up to date?

41
00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.140
And the answer is really opportunistic moments.

42
00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.640
Whether they're coming into the pharmacy for a flu shot, a medication review, or

43
00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.400
they're just collecting their insulin, a quick chat at the counter

44
00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:04.420
could make all the difference. Or potentially, they're coming into the GP clinic,

45
00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:08.310
and they're doing a kidney health plan, or that you're looking into other

46
00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.980
chronic diseases, or even a health assessment.

47
00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.840
Taking the time to ask, "Do you have a sick day plan?" If

48
00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:17.640
not, that's your opening to start.

49
00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.360
Now, remember, plans don't have to be perfect, and that there's

50
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.980
lots of different plans out there.

51
00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.740
And depending on the person, you could just start simply.

52
00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.020
For example, we've got here just a two-page plan, and this can

53
00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:34.300
be a really great entry point and can be refined over

54
00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:38.350
time. Ideally, it'd be great to review it every year or

55
00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:41.720
even more frequently as a person's health status changes.
